ML Model
Cell 12 – Train Sentiment Classification Model
Purpose: This cell trains a Machine Learning model using customer reviews to classify them into Positive, 
Negative and Neutral sentiments. It then predicts sentiments for test reviews and evaluates the model's performance.
Short explanation for presentation
"This cell develops a sentiment classification model using PySpark Machine Learning.

First, we split the balanced dataset into 80% training data and 20% testing data.

Next, we preprocess the review text using tokenization, stop-word removal 
and TF-IDF to convert text into numerical features.

We convert sentiment labels into numerical values using StringIndexer and use Logistic 
Regression as our classification algorithm.

All these steps are combined into a Machine Learning pipeline. We train the model using
the training dataset and make predictions on the testing dataset.

Finally, we evaluate the model using Accuracy, Precision, Recall and F1 Score
and display the confusion matrix to understand its classification performance."

# ==========================================
# CELL 12: TRAIN SENTIMENT CLASSIFICATION MODEL
# ==========================================

from pyspark.ml import Pipeline
from pyspark.ml.feature import (
    RegexTokenizer,
    StopWordsRemover,
    HashingTF,
    IDF,
    StringIndexer
)
from pyspark.ml.classification import LogisticRegression
from pyspark.ml.evaluation import MulticlassClassificationEvaluator

# STEP 1: Split data into training and testing
train_df, test_df = balanced_df.randomSplit(
    [0.8, 0.2],
    seed=42
)

print("Training records:", train_df.count())
print("Testing records:", test_df.count())

# STEP 2: Tokenize review text
tokenizer = RegexTokenizer(
    inputCol="clean_review",
    outputCol="words",
    pattern="\\W+"
)

# STEP 3: Remove common words
remover = StopWordsRemover(
    inputCol="words",
    outputCol="filtered_words"
)

# STEP 4: Convert words into numerical features
hashing = HashingTF(
    inputCol="filtered_words",
    outputCol="raw_features",
    numFeatures=65536
)

# STEP 5: Apply TF-IDF
idf = IDF(
    inputCol="raw_features",
    outputCol="features"
)

# STEP 6: Convert sentiment labels into numerical values
label_indexer = StringIndexer(
    inputCol="sentiment",
    outputCol="label"
)

# STEP 7: Create Logistic Regression classifier
lr = LogisticRegression(
    featuresCol="features",
    labelCol="label",
    maxIter=30,
    regParam=0.01
)

# STEP 8: Build complete ML pipeline
pipeline = Pipeline(stages=[
    tokenizer,
    remover,
    hashing,
    idf,
    label_indexer,
    lr
])

# STEP 9: Train the model
print("\nTraining sentiment model...")

sentiment_model = pipeline.fit(train_df)

print("Model training completed successfully!")

# STEP 10: Make predictions
predictions = sentiment_model.transform(test_df)

print("\nSample predictions:")

predictions.select(
    "clean_review",
    "sentiment",
    "prediction"
).show(10, truncate=100)

# STEP 11: Evaluate model performance
evaluator = MulticlassClassificationEvaluator(
    labelCol="label",
    predictionCol="prediction",
    metricName="accuracy"
)

accuracy = evaluator.evaluate(predictions)

f1_evaluator = MulticlassClassificationEvaluator(
    labelCol="label",
    predictionCol="prediction",
    metricName="f1"
)

f1_score = f1_evaluator.evaluate(predictions)

precision_evaluator = MulticlassClassificationEvaluator(
    labelCol="label",
    predictionCol="prediction",
    metricName="weightedPrecision"
)

precision = precision_evaluator.evaluate(predictions)

recall_evaluator = MulticlassClassificationEvaluator(
    labelCol="label",
    predictionCol="prediction",
    metricName="weightedRecall"
)

recall = recall_evaluator.evaluate(predictions)

print("\n========== MODEL PERFORMANCE ==========")
print(f"Accuracy  : {accuracy * 100:.2f}%")
print(f"Precision : {precision * 100:.2f}%")
print(f"Recall    : {recall * 100:.2f}%")
print(f"F1 Score  : {f1_score * 100:.2f}%")

# STEP 12: Confusion matrix
print("\nConfusion Matrix:")

predictions.groupBy(
    "label",
    "prediction"
).count().orderBy(
    "label",
    "prediction"
).show()
